 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Cecilie Bahnsen’s dresses have never suffered from a lack of romance. This season, however, they seemed to have discovered the outside world. The prettiness remained, along with volume and enough embroidery and appliqués to make a Victorian heroine feel underdressed, but the clothes moved, slouched and collapsed, occasionally looking as though they had wandered in from the street.</p><p>Bahnsen called the collection Any Given Day, with the intention of dressing women from eight in the morning to eight at night, with enough ease to survive the hours in between. “I wanted a softness, but still with movement and life,” she said. The show itself made the point literally, with models entering from the street, passing through the newly renovated Richelieu space and heading back out again.</p><p>Despite their conspicuous embellishments, the pieces were not presented as precious objects so much as things with somewhere to go. That meant slimmer, longer silhouettes, with Bahnsen’s familiar volumes pulled in, dropped down or made detachable. A long lace gown could be followed by a slouchy silk T-shirt; a romantic dress might be broken down into a bralette, T-shirt and skirt with the ease of a skater girl. Italian denim and softened workwear grounded the frothier pieces, while liquid silk and nightwear slipped into daylight. Scalloped French lace was translated into Bahnsen’s intricate sequined and beaded textures. A translucent technical nylon introduced a useful dose of reality into the fantasy.</p><p>Even the architecture of the clothes was made negotiable. Crinolines and panniers exaggerated the hips beneath softer layers, but could be removed or adjusted with drawstrings. Transparency, cut-outs and layering gave the body more room to participate.</p><p>The Scandinavian restraint and French romanticism were still there, but the balance had shifted toward what Bahnsen called “ease and nonchalance.” There was no prescribed Cecilie Bahnsen woman, either. “One woman makes it romantic, another makes it tough,” she said. “The clothes don’t dictate the character, the woman does.” A gown could go out for the day; workwear could flirt with nightwear; a pannier could be taken off before dinner. Bahnsen hasn’t abandoned fantasy, she has simply given it a busier schedule.</p></div>",
